description:
## Problem

`tui.editor.jumpForward` (`ctrl+]`) and `tui.editor.jumpBackward` (`ctrl+alt+]`) are **not in our `OctoPi.TUI.Keybindings` defaults** and not implemented in the editor. The keys parse correctly per telemetry trace (`parsed=%OctoPi.TUI.Key{key: 93, modifiers: [:ctrl]}` and `[:ctrl, :alt]`), so the gap is purely registry + handler.

## Behavior (stateful — unlike every other binding)

From upstream `tmp/pi-mono/packages/tui/src/components/editor.ts:538-556, 796-803, 1943-1971`:

1. Pressing `ctrl+]` sets `jumpMode = "forward"`. Pressing `ctrl+alt+]` sets `jumpMode = "backward"`.
2. The **next keystroke** is consumed: if it's a printable character, the editor jumps the cursor to the next/previous occurrence of that char in the document. If it's a control character or the same hotkey again, jump mode cancels.
3. `jumpToChar` searches forward/backward starting from `cursor+1` / `cursor-1`, walking lines until a match is found or the document ends. No match → cursor unchanged.

Upstream `jumpToChar` (`editor.ts:1943-1971`):

```ts
private jumpToChar(char: string, direction: "forward" | "backward"): void {
  this.lastAction = null;
  const isForward = direction === "forward";
  const lines = this.state.lines;
  const end = isForward ? lines.length : -1;
  const step = isForward ? 1 : -1;
  for (let lineIdx = this.state.cursorLine; lineIdx !== end; lineIdx += step) {
    const line = lines[lineIdx] || "";
    const isCurrentLine = lineIdx === this.state.cursorLine;
    const searchFrom = isCurrentLine
      ? (isForward ? this.state.cursorCol + 1 : this.state.cursorCol - 1)
      : undefined;
    const idx = isForward ? line.indexOf(char, searchFrom) : line.lastIndexOf(char, searchFrom);
    if (idx !== -1) {
      this.state.cursorLine = lineIdx;
      this.setCursorCol(idx);
      return;
    }
  }
}
```

## Files to modify

- `apps/octo_pi_tui/lib/octo_pi/tui/keybindings.ex` — add defaults:
  ```elixir
  "tui.editor.jumpForward" => %{keys: ["ctrl+]"], description: "Jump forward to character"},
  "tui.editor.jumpBackward" => %{keys: ["ctrl+alt+]"], description: "Jump backward to character"},
  ```
- The Elixir editor module currently handling `tui.editor.cursorUp` etc. — locate via grep `"tui.editor.cursorUp"` in `apps/octo_pi_tui/lib/`. Add a `:jump_mode` field to its state (`:forward | :backward | nil`), and a "consume next printable char" path that intercepts before the normal handler.

## Acceptance

- Defaults entries added.
- Pressing `ctrl+]` then a printable char moves cursor to the next occurrence on or after `cursor+1`; if no match, cursor stays.
- Pressing `ctrl+]` twice cancels jump mode (no jump happens, second press is a no-op).
- Pressing `ctrl+]` then a control character cancels jump mode and that control char's normal handler runs.
- Backward variant (`ctrl+alt+]`) symmetric.
- Tests cover the three branches (match / no match / cancel-by-rehotkey / cancel-by-control-char).

## Notes

This is the only stateful binding in the system. All other bindings are one-shot. The implementation needs editor state, not just a key handler. When porting, the upstream pattern of `jumpMode = null` early-cancel before falling through to the rest of `handleInput` is the cleanest approach.
